            <ArticleTitle>[Mondor's disease: a complication after breast surgery].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>197-201</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Mondor's disease is a rare but not uncommon complication of breast surgery. This problem is commonly described as the thrombophlebitis of the superficial thoracoabdominal veins. Symptoms combine painful contracture occurring in the sub-mammary region and/or in the axillary region, rising during arm abduction. The contracture located in the axillary region usually join the epitrochlea. Based on a serie of 8 patients, the fascial hypothesis is developed. All the patients were treated by LPG and myo-fascial techiques with a complete relief of pain in 10 days.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
